\tag{discrete}
\tag{draft}
\taxon{Example}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{[[002Z]] works just like we expect in natural language. Consider:
   "It is not raining today and I had eggs for breakfast."}

\p{This is true if both parts are true.  If it's raining, the
   statement is false.  If I ate something other than eggs, it's
   false.  Of course it's definitely false when it's raining and I ate
   something other than eggs.}

\p{If we let #{r} = "It is raining today" and #{e} = "I
   had eggs for breakfast", then we can translate this sentence
   into propositional logic as ##{\neg r \land e.} Note that
   negation has "higher precedence" than conjunction (it has
   "stickier glue") so this is unambiguous.  It does \em{not} mean
   #{\neg(r \land e)}, which would be different.  If in doubt, just use
   parentheses: #{(\neg r) \land e}.}
